hypotheses.statement:
For every integer $d\ge3$ there are constants $\alpha_d\in(0,1/3]$ and $C_d<\infty$ such that every bounded infinity-harmonic function $u\in C(B_1)$, where $B_1\subset\mathbb{R}^d$, belongs to $C^{1,\alpha_d}(B_{1/2})$ and satisfies $$\|\nabla u\|_{L^\infty(B_{1/2})}
       +[\nabla u]_{C^{0,\alpha_d}(B_{1/2})}
       \le C_d\mathop{\mathrm{osc}}_{B_1}u.$$

Let $U_i$ be infinity-harmonic on $B_1\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ with a common Lipschitz bound, and let $\ell_i$ be affine functions such that $$\|U_i-\ell_i\|_{L^\infty(B_1)}\longrightarrow0.$$ Then $$\label{eq:three-point}
  \sup_{\substack{x,y\in B_{1/4}\\x\ne y}}
  \frac{|U_i(x)+U_i(y)-2U_i((x+y)/2)|}{|x-y|}
  \longrightarrow0.$$

Let $m\ge1$ and $0\le C_0<\infty$. Suppose that $v\in C(\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R})$ is a viscosity solution of $$\label{eq:limit-pde}
  F[v]:=D^2v[(\nabla_xv,1)]=0.$$ Suppose, in addition, that for every $z=(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}$ and every $R>0$ there are $p\in\mathbb{R}^m$ and $a\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $$\label{eq:limit-fit}
  \left|v(x+h,t+\ell)-v(x,t)-p\cdot h
       -\left(a-\frac{|p|^2}{2}\right)\ell\right|\le C_0R
  \quad\text{if }\quad |\ell|\le R,\quad |h-p\ell|\le R.$$ Then $v$ is affine.

The family modulus in <a href="#eq:flatness-function" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:flatness-function">[eq:flatness-function]</a> satisfies $$\label{eq:family-flatness}
  b(s)\longrightarrow0\qquad(s\downarrow0).$$ Every cylinder fit satisfies $$\label{eq:physical-slope-bounds}
  |P|\le L+\sqrt\lambda,\qquad
  |P\cdot e_b-1|\le\sqrt\lambda.$$

Fix $0<c_*\le C_*<\infty$. Suppose two fits for the same function, of the form <a href="#eq:physical-fit" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:physical-fit">[eq:physical-fit]</a>, with parameter $\theta^2\in(0,1)$, have slopes $P,P'$ satisfying $c_*\le |P|,|P'|\le C_*$. Write $n=P/|P|$ and $n'=P'/|P'|$. If their centers agree and their radii are $H$ and $H'$ with $H\le H'\le2H$, then $$\label{eq:cylinder-comparison}
  |P-P'|+|n-n'|\le C\theta,\qquad
  \bigl||P|-|P'|\bigr|\le C\theta^2,$$ where $C$ depends only on $c_*,C_*$. The same conclusion holds for equal radii $H$ at centers $z,z'$ if $z'$ lies in the first cylinder with axial and transverse offsets at most $H/4$ and $\theta H/4$, respectively.

Let $v\in\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$, $z_0=(x_0,t_0)$, $R>0$, and $(p_0,a_0)\in\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}$. Then $$\label{eq:frame-change}
  \bar v(y,s)=
  \frac{v(x_0+R(y+p_0s),t_0+Rs)-v(x_0,t_0)}{R}
       -p_0\cdot y-\left(a_0+\frac{|p_0|^2}{2}\right)s$$ belongs to $\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$. A fit $(p,a)$ for $v$ at the corresponding center and radius $R\rho$ becomes the fit $(p-p_0,a-a_0)$ for $\bar v$ at radius $\rho$.

Suppose that $v\in\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$ is centered at either $(0,0)$ or $(0,1)$. For $K\ge2$ one has $$\label{eq:centered-growth}
  |v(x,t)|\le C K L(K)^2\qquad ((x,t)\in\mathcal B_K).$$ Every fit $(p,a)$ at a center in $\mathcal B_K$, of any radius $r>0$, satisfies $$\label{eq:fit-parameter-bound}
  |p|+|a|\le C\bigl(L(K)+|\log r|\bigr).$$ In particular, for a center $(x,t)\in\mathcal B_K$, $$\begin{aligned}
  |v(x+h,t)-v(x,t)|
    &\le Cr\bigl(L(K)+|\log r|\bigr)
       &&(|h|\le r),\label{eq:spatial-fit-modulus}\\
  |v(x,t+\tau)-v(x,t)|
    &\le C|\tau|\bigl(L(K)+|\log|\tau||\bigr)^2
       &&(0<|\tau|\le\tfrac12).\label{eq:time-fit-modulus}
\end{aligned}$$ For either fixed choice of center, every sequence of centered members has a locally uniformly convergent subsequence, and its limit belongs to $\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$ and is centered at that same point. Moreover, on every compact time interval these members have uniformly subquadratic growth in $x$: $$\label{eq:uniform-subquadratic-growth}
  \lim_{M\to\infty}\ 
  \sup_{\substack{v\text{ centered}\\ |x|\ge M,\ t\in J}}
       \frac{|v(x,t)|}{|x|^2}=0
  \qquad(J\Subset\mathbb{R}).$$

Suppose that $f$ has the preceding growth property. The extrema in <a href="#eq:envelope-definitions" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:envelope-definitions">[eq:envelope-definitions]</a> are attained. The envelopes are continuous jointly in their center and length, including length zero wherever the time arguments remain in $I$. Whenever the expressions are defined, $$\label{eq:envelope-composition}
  \mathcal{P}_\ell(\mathcal{P}_hf)=\mathcal{P}_{\ell+h}f,\qquad
  \mathcal{M}_\ell(\mathcal{M}_hf)=\mathcal{M}_{\ell+h}f.$$ If $f$ is a viscosity subsolution of <a href="#eq:limit-pde" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:limit-pde">[eq:limit-pde]</a>, then, for every affine path $x(\ell)=x_0+d\ell$ and every fixed $t$, $$\label{eq:path-envelope-convexity}
  \ell\longmapsto
  \mathcal{P}_\ell f(x(\ell),t)+|d|^2\ell\log\ell
  \quad\text{is convex}$$ on its allowed range $\ell\ge0$, with $\ell\log\ell=0$ at zero. In particular $\ell\mapsto\mathcal{P}_\ell f(x,t)$ is convex. If $f$ is a viscosity supersolution, then $\ell\mapsto\mathcal{M}_\ell f(x,t)$ is concave. If $f_j\in C(\mathbb{R}^m\times I)$, $f_j\to f$ locally uniformly, and $$\lim_{M\to\infty}\sup_j\sup_{\substack{|y|\ge M\\t\in J}}
      \frac{|f_j(y,t)|}{|y|^2}=0\qquad(J\Subset I),$$ then $\mathcal{P}_\ell f_j\to\mathcal{P}_\ell f$ and $\mathcal{M}_\ell f_j\to\mathcal{M}_\ell f$ locally uniformly in the allowed centers and positive lengths.

For a member $v\in\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$ centered at $(0,0)$ or $(0,1)$, $K\ge2$, $z\in\mathcal B_K$, and $0<\ell\le K$, one has $$\label{eq:envelope-slope-bound}
  \mathcal{S}_\ell^+v(z)\le C L(K)^4,\qquad
  \mathcal{S}_\ell^-v(z)\le C L(K)^4.$$ Every optimizing displacement for $\mathcal{P}_Kv(z)$ or $\mathcal{M}_Kv(z)$ has length at most $C K L(K)^2$.

Let $f$ have the preceding growth property on $\mathbb{R}^m\times I$. For $s>0$ the supremum in <a href="#eq:barycentric-envelope" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:barycentric-envelope">[eq:barycentric-envelope]</a> is attained by a finite configuration, and $$\label{eq:barycentric-duality}
  \mathcal{C}_s f(x,t)-\frac{|x|^2}{2s}
   =\min_{A\in\mathbb{R}^m}\ \max_{y\in\mathbb{R}^m}
       \left\{f(y,t)-\frac{|y|^2}{2s}-A\cdot(y-x)\right\}.$$ Both extrema on the right are attained. Allowing any probability measure of finite second moment and mean $x$ in <a href="#eq:barycentric-envelope" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:barycentric-envelope">[eq:barycentric-envelope]</a> gives the same value. The function $\mathcal{C}_s f(x,t)$ is continuous in $(x,t,s)$ for $s>0$, and $$\label{eq:barycentric-semiconcavity}
  x\longmapsto\mathcal{C}_s f(x,t)-\frac{|x|^2}{2s}
       \quad\text{is concave}.$$ If $f_j\in C(\mathbb{R}^m\times I)$, $f_j\to f$ locally uniformly, and the sequence has the common growth control $$\lim_{M\to\infty}
  \sup_j\sup_{\substack{|y|\ge M\\t\in J}}
       \frac{|f_j(y,t)|}{|y|^2}=0
       \qquad(J\Subset I),$$ then $\mathcal{C}_s f_j\to\mathcal{C}_s f$ locally uniformly for $s>0$.

If $\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$ contains a nonaffine member, then there exist $\kappa\in(0,\infty)$ and $v\in\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$ centered at $z_*=(0,1)$ for which <a href="#eq:jensen-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:jensen-bound">[eq:jensen-bound]</a> holds with $f=v$ and $I=\mathbb{R}$. For $t>0$ set $$\label{eq:contact-envelope}
  W(x,t)=\mathcal{C}_t v(x,t)-\frac{\kappa^2t}{2}.$$ This function is continuous, has subquadratic spatial growth on compact positive-time intervals, and is a viscosity subsolution of <a href="#eq:limit-pde" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:limit-pde">[eq:limit-pde]</a> on $\mathbb{R}^m\times(0,\infty)$. It satisfies $$\label{eq:contact-sandwich}
  v(x,t)-\frac{\kappa^2t}{2}\le W(x,t)\le v(x,t)
       \quad(t>0),\qquad W(z_*)=v(z_*)=0,$$ and $W(\cdot,t)-|x|^2/(2t)$ is concave for every $t>0$. At every point of contact with $t>0$, the barycentric gap satisfies $$\label{eq:contact-barycentric-gap}
  W(x,t)=v(x,t)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  \frac{\mathcal{C}_t v(x,t)-v(x,t)}{t}=\frac{\kappa^2}{2}>0.$$ Finally, $$\label{eq:contact-slope}
  H_v(z_*)\in\mathbb{R},\qquad
  \lim_{\ell\downarrow0}\mathcal{S}_\ell^+W(z_*)=H_v(z_*).$$

For every $t,\ell>0$, the optimization defining $f^\ell(x,t)$ has a unique maximizer $y=y(x,t,\ell)$. The function $f^\ell(\cdot,t)$ is differentiable, and $$\label{eq:dp-gradient}
  \nabla_x f^\ell(x,t)=\frac{y-x}{\ell}.$$ For every $h\in\mathbb{R}^m$ it satisfies the two supporting inequalities $$\label{eq:dp-supports}
\begin{split}
  f^\ell(x,t)+\nabla_x f^\ell(x,t)\cdot h-\frac{|h|^2}{2\ell}
  &\le f^\ell(x+h,t)\\
  &\le f^\ell(x,t)+\nabla_x f^\ell(x,t)\cdot h+\frac{|h|^2}{2t}.
\end{split}$$ In particular, its spatial Hessian is bounded below by $-\ell^{-1}I$ in the semiconvexity sense and above by $t^{-1}I$ in the semiconcavity sense.

Let $r>0$, and let $(p_0,a_0)$ be a fit for $v$ at $(x_0,r)$ of radius $r$. At corresponding points define $$\label{eq:contact-frame}
\begin{gathered}
  s=\frac{t}{r},\qquad y=\frac{x-x_0-p_0(t-r)}{r},\\
  x=x_0+r\bigl(y+p_0(s-1)\bigr),\qquad t=rs,\\
  \bar v(y,s)=\frac{v(x,t)-v(x_0,r)}{r}
          -p_0\cdot y-\left(a_0+\frac{|p_0|^2}{2}\right)(s-1).
\end{gathered}$$ Then $\bar v\in\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$ is centered at $(0,1)$ and satisfies <a href="#eq:jensen-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:jensen-bound">[eq:jensen-bound]</a> with $f=\bar v$, $I=\mathbb{R}$, and the same $\kappa$. Define $$\bar W(y,s)=\mathcal{C}_s\bar v(y,s)-\frac{\kappa^2s}{2}\qquad(s>0).$$ For every $\sigma>0$, the barycentric and $W$ gaps satisfy $$\label{eq:contact-W-covariance}
\begin{split}
  \mathcal{C}_\sigma\bar v(y,s)-\bar v(y,s)
       &=\frac{\mathcal{C}_{r\sigma}v(x,t)-v(x,t)}{r},\\
  \bar W(y,s)-\bar v(y,s)&=\frac{W(x,t)-v(x,t)}{r}
       \qquad(s>0).
\end{split}$$ For every $\ell>0$, the solution slopes satisfy $$\label{eq:fitted-slope-covariance}
  \mathcal{S}_\ell^\pm\bar v(y,s)=\mathcal{S}_{r\ell}^\pm v(x,t)-a_0.$$ If the steps in <a href="#eq:dp-step-choice" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:dp-step-choice">[eq:dp-step-choice]</a> are chosen at any state with $t>\epsilon$, their images are optimizing steps for the transformed pair, with step size $\epsilon'=\epsilon/r$. Their velocities satisfy $$\label{eq:contact-velocity-covariance}
\begin{gathered}
  Q'_+=\frac{y^+-y}{\epsilon'}=Q_+-p_0,\qquad
  Q'_- =\frac{y-y^-}{\epsilon'}=Q_--p_0,\\
  Q'_0=\nabla_y\mathcal{P}_{\epsilon'}\bar W(y,s)=Q_0-p_0,\qquad
  Q'_\pm-Q'_0=Q_\pm-Q_0.
\end{gathered}$$ All these identities are global at corresponding states, with the indicated positive-time restriction for $W$; the states need not lie in the fitting box. No contact at $(x_0,r)$ is assumed. The centering and fit assertion concerns $\bar v$ alone.

Suppose $K\ge2$, $|x|,|t|\le K$, $0<\epsilon\le1$, and $t>\epsilon$. Then the velocity in <a href="#eq:dp-velocities" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:dp-velocities">[eq:dp-velocities]</a> satisfies $$\label{eq:dp-log-velocity}
  |Q_0|=|\nabla_x\mathcal{P}_\epsilon W(x,t)|
       \le C\bigl(L(K)+|\log t|\bigr).$$ Here $C$ depends only on the fitting constant, the spatial dimension, and $\kappa$.

Put $\delta_j=2^{-j}$ for $j\ge1$. There exist a constant $K<\infty$, a point $x_{\rm end}\in\mathbb{R}^m$, and an infinite set $\mathcal J\subset\mathbb N$ with the following property. For every $j\in\mathcal J$ there are a point $x_j\in\mathbb{R}^m$ and a fit $(p_j,a_j)$ for $v$ at $(x_j,\delta_j)$ of radius $\delta_j$, in the sense of <a href="#eq:limit-fit" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:limit-fit">[eq:limit-fit]</a>, such that $$\label{eq:contact-propagation-conclusion}
\begin{gathered}
  H_v(x_{\rm end},0)>-\infty,\qquad
  W(x_j,\delta_j)=v(x_j,\delta_j),\\
  |x_{\rm end}-x_j+p_j\delta_j|\le K\delta_j.
\end{gathered}$$

Let $v\in\mathcal{A}_{m,C_0}$, with the class and envelopes defined in Section <a href="#sec:limiting-tools" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:limiting-tools">4</a>. Suppose that for some $(b,t_0)\in\mathbb{R}^m\times\mathbb{R}$ and $B\in\mathbb{R}$, $$\label{eq:constant-envelope-slopes}
  \mathcal{S}_\ell^+v(b,t_0)=\mathcal{S}_\ell^-v(b,t_0)=B
       \qquad\text{for every }\ell>0.$$ Then $v$ is affine.

